{"name":"Paradise Green","hex":"#a3e493","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"paradise","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The place where sanctified souls are believed to live after death.","A very pleasant place, such as a place full of lush vegetation."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Paradise (“Heaven”).","Alternative letter-case form of Paradise (“the Garden of Eden”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/paradise"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Paradise","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and folklore, paradise is a place of everlasting happiness, delight, and bliss. Paradisiacal notions are often laden with pastoral imagery, and may be cosmogonical, eschatological, or both, often contrasted with the miseries of human civilization: in paradise there is only peace, prosperity, and happiness. Paradise is a place of contentment, a land of luxury and fulfillment containing ever-lasting bliss and delight. Paradise is often described as a \"higher place\", the holiest place, in contrast to this world, or underworlds such as hell."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg/330px-Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise","wikidata":"Q3363340"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg/500px-Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg/500px-Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Last Judgment (detail), c.1431, by Fra Angelico depicting people in paradise","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAngelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/500px-Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/1280px-Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/500px-Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The luxurious palace and gardens of Neo-Assyrian king Ashurbanipal (ruled 668–631 BCE) at Nineveh, with original color reconstitution. Irrigation canals radiate from an aqueduct. The king appears under the porch. British Museum.[8][9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg/500px-Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg/1280px-Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg/500px-Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Paradise by Jan Bruegel, circa 1620","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg/500px-Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg/1280px-Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg/500px-Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nicolas Poussin, Four seasons of paradise, 1660–166